## The late funding fee

The late funding fee is booked on a program's most recent regular payroll batch, the regular batch with the latest pay date, when that batch's funds arrived after its funding deadline; it is debited from the funding balance as a fee of the `late_funding` kind pointing to the batch and carrying its pay date, and a batch whose funds arrived in time carries none.

For a program's most recent regular payroll batch, and only for that batch, funds arriving after the deadline are still credited and the batch is charged the late funding fee figured for that batch; they post to cards on the pay date only if they arrive before the package's load posting time that day, and otherwise on the next business day; every other batch remains under the base rule, which reads: Funds arriving after that deadline are still credited, and the batch is charged the package's late funding fee; they post to cards on the pay date only if they arrive before the package's load posting time that day, and otherwise on the next business day.

This applies to every Paystride Payroll Card Program, whatever its package.

## Debiting a program fee

Program fees are debited with `debit_program_fee_609ed260`, an agent-side tool: the agent unlocks it and then calls it.

**Capability.** It debits one program fee from the funding account of an employer's Payroll Card Program. One fee entry of the kind given is added to the program's funding account history, dated today, pointing to the funding account entry that incurred it and carrying that entry's pay date when it has one, and the program's funding balance falls by the fee. A fee of zero, or a call naming no fee kind, debits nothing and leaves the program unchanged. The employer sees the fee entry and the reduced funding balance in the funding history.

### Parameters

- `paycard_program_id` — the reference number of the employer's Payroll Card Program, as returned by the program lookup for this employer (identifier).
- `related_entry_id` — the reference number of the funding account entry that incurred the fee (the payroll batch, funding receipt, kit order or funding return), as shown in the program's funding account history (identifier).
- `fee_type` — the kind of fee debited, exactly one of:
  - `monthly_program` — the flat monthly program fee of a billing month;
  - `active_card` — the monthly fee for the cards loaded in a billing month;
  - `card_kit` — the kit fee of an instant-issue card kit order;
  - `kit_shipping` — the standard shipping of a kit order;
  - `expedited_kit_shipping` — the expedited shipping of a kit order;
  - `late_funding` — the fee on a payroll batch whose funds arrived after its funding deadline;
  - `returned_funding` — the fee on a funding payment returned unpaid;
  - `off_cycle_load` — the fee on an off-cycle batch beyond the month's free allowance;
  - `load_file_correction` — the fee for correcting an accepted load file;
  - `load_reversal` — the fee on a load reversal request;
  - `reinstatement` — the fee on reinstating a suspended program;
  - `early_termination` — the fee on terminating within the minimum term;
  - `none` — no fee kind, which books nothing.
- `fee_amount_usd` — the fee in US dollars, zero or a positive number with 2 decimal places; a value with more places is rounded half up to the cent before it is booked.

### Return

- A line beginning "Program fee debited" indicates the fee was booked; it names the fee entry reference (a text identifier), the fee kind, the fee debited (US dollars, two decimal places), the source entry reference and its pay date (YYYY-MM-DD, empty when it has none), and the funding balance after the debit (US dollars, two decimal places).
- A line beginning "No program fee debited" reports a call that changed nothing.

### Contract illustration (hypothetical)

A call with `fee_type` `late_funding`, a `related_entry_id` naming a payroll batch and `fee_amount_usd` of 41.235 books 41.24 pointing to that batch with the batch's pay date; the same call with `fee_amount_usd` of 0 returns a line beginning "No program fee debited".
